        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2024-11-30T08:13:05Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "No worries, the documentation probably could be a bit more explicit here.\r\n\r\n`<CR>` means `Enter` key and `<BS>` means `Backspace` key. It is a standard notation for those keys in Neovim help. You can look at `:h key-notation` for more details.\r\n\r\nRegistering pair for the key means that pressing that key when inside the pair will do something extra (see [here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c8922aef8207137e66c80acdb9523668599ba62a/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L165-L168)):\r\n- Pressing `<CR>` inside registered pair (for example, `()`) leads to putting an extra blank indented line between pair characters. See [here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c8922aef8207137e66c80acdb9523668599ba62a/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L324-L328).\r\n- Pressing `<BS>` inside registered pair (for example, `()`) leads to deleting whole pair. See [here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c8922aef8207137e66c80acdb9523668599ba62a/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L294-L298).\r\n\r\nHope this helps.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">No worries, the documentation probably could be a bit more explicit here.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;CR&gt;</code> means <code class=\"notranslate\">Enter</code> key and <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;BS&gt;</code> means <code class=\"notranslate\">Backspace</code> key. It is a standard notation for those keys in Neovim help. You can look at <code class=\"notranslate\">:h key-notation</code> for more details.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Registering pair for the key means that pressing that key when inside the pair will do something extra (see <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c8922aef8207137e66c80acdb9523668599ba62a/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L165-L168\">here</a>):</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li>Pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;CR&gt;</code> inside registered pair (for example, <code class=\"notranslate\">()</code>) leads to putting an extra blank indented line between pair characters. See <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c8922aef8207137e66c80acdb9523668599ba62a/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L324-L328\">here</a>.</li>\n<li>Pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;BS&gt;</code> inside registered pair (for example, <code class=\"notranslate\">()</code>) leads to deleting whole pair. See <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c8922aef8207137e66c80acdb9523668599ba62a/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L294-L298\">here</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Hope this helps.</p>",
            "bodyText": "No worries, the documentation probably could be a bit more explicit here.\n<CR> means Enter key and <BS> means Backspace key. It is a standard notation for those keys in Neovim help. You can look at :h key-notation for more details.\nRegistering pair for the key means that pressing that key when inside the pair will do something extra (see here):\n\nPressing <CR> inside registered pair (for example, ()) leads to putting an extra blank indented line between pair characters. See here.\nPressing <BS> inside registered pair (for example, ()) leads to deleting whole pair. See here.\n\nHope this helps.",
